data.content:
**What happened:** C# 14 introduces new `Span<T>` conversions. When you write `array.Contains(num)` inside an expression tree, the compiler now binds to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`. Expression trees cannot represent `Span<T>`-based methods, so this throws at runtime.

**Fix — cast or call explicitly:**

```csharp
// Option 1: .AsEnumerable() to force IEnumerable<T> binding
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Both approaches ensure the compiler picks the `Enumerable.Contains` overload, which is representable in expression trees.
